This port is for the Dragon 64 with SAMx8 memory expansion.  It uses the 16K
banks memory model with flexible mapping.

The SAMx8 has COMMON flags to fix either or both of the top 4K regions in place
as being from page 31, so we're using the very top one for the common area.

Video RAM has been squeezed into the lower addresses of the same page, so
bank16k can be told that all of pages 4--30 are available for user processes.

The CPU vectors at $FFFx are taken from the same page in COMMON mode, so while
they're read-only at that address, they can be modified by mapping page 31
elsewhere.

RAM top set to F000.  Video mapped to $C000+ while modifying.

It's expected that a boot loader will position the kernel within pages 0--3.
One of the first things the start code does is copy the common area from page 3
to page 31.

Like the MOOH and the GIME, the SAMx8 has two tasks, so data copying is mostly
restricted to stashing the udata per-process (hidden "behind" the real udata in
process's top page).  The kernel runs from task 1, userspace from task 0.

Much of the code started off in the MOOH (Tormod Volden) and CoCo 3 (Brett
Gordon) platforms.

TTY allocation:

tty1 is 256x192 RG6 bitmaps graphics mode using 8x8 font
tty2 is 32x16 SG4 text mode
tty3, tty4 reserved
tty5 is 6551 ACIA (tested working @ 9600 baud)
tty6, tty7 for DriveWire (untested)


RUNNING ON REAL HARDWARE
========================

You'll need a Dragon 64 with SAMx8 fitted, and a CoCoSDC.

# Build the 'sdc' subtarget with CoCoSDC support
$ make TARGET=dragon-samx8 SUBTARGET=sdc diskimage

Copy "Images/dragon-samx8/fuzix-sdc.img" to your SD card, possibly as
"FUZIX.VHD".

On your Dragon, mount the VHD file (assuming Pere Serrat's DOSplus extender):

SDRIVE1"FUZIX.VHD"

Then type BOOT.  Type 'hda1' at the boot prompt.


RUNNING UNDER EMULATION
=======================

We build a bootstrap floppy image which contains a bootloader and the kernel.
Once the kernel starts, it has support to mount the real Fuzix image over
DriveWire.

# Build the 'emu' subtarget with DriveWire support
$ make TARGET=dragon-samx8 SUBTARGET=emu diskimage

# Serve the HD image over DriveWire.  You can use a more user-friendly
# DriveWire server if you like.
$ socat TCP4-LISTEN:65504,nodelay,fork,bind=127.0.0.1 SYSTEM:"lwwire drive=0\\,Images/dragon-samx8/filesys.img 2>/dev/null" &

# Run XRoar as a Dragon 64 with SAMx8 fitted, with a "Becker port" enabled
# DragonDOS cartridge.
$ xroar -m dragon64 -machine-opt sam=samx8 -cart dragondos -cart-becker Images/dragon-samx8/fuzix-ddos.DSK

XRoar should automatically type "BOOT" to boot off the mounted floppy.  Type
'dw' at the boot prompt.  Remember when you finish that you have a socat job
running in the background.


